---
import LearningGuideLayout from "../../layouts/LearningGuideLayout.astro";
import GuideFigure from "../../components/GuideFigure.astro";
---

<LearningGuideLayout
  title="How to Switch Cameras and Create Cutscenes from Blender"
  description="Author Blender cameras and use FPE Scene Events to switch Godot views, pause player controls, and return safely after a cutscene."
  summary="Artists compose shots. Put the camera where the reveal looks best in Blender, then connect that shot to the same triggers, animation beats, and level events that give the scene its rhythm."
  relatedSlugs={[
    "create-triggers-in-blender-for-godot",
    "animation-frame-events-godot",
    "change-scenes-load-levels-godot",
  ]}
  godotSource={{
    label: "Camera3D class reference",
    href: "https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_camera3d.html",
  }}
  fpeHeading="What FPE creates in Godot"
>
  <div class="guide-summary">
    <strong>You will make:</strong> a Blender-authored cutaway camera activated by
    a Scene Event, with an explicit return to the player camera and controls.
  </div>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/blender/camera-composition-view.png"
    alt="GardenRevealCamera selected in Blender while looking through its composed frame"
    caption="Select the camera and enter Camera View to compose the shot against the actual level geometry."
  />
  <h2>Compose the shot in Blender</h2><ol>
    <li>
      Add and name the camera for its purpose, such as <code
        >GardenRevealCamera</code
      >.
    </li><li>
      Frame the shot with the same level geometry and character placement the
      player will see.
    </li><li>
      Create a trigger or animation Frame Event for the moment the shot should
      begin.
    </li><li>
      In the matching Scene Event, add <strong
        >Deactivate Player Controls</strong
      > and <strong>Activate Camera</strong>.
    </li><li>
      At the end, add <strong>Reactivate Player Camera</strong> and <strong
        >Reactivate Player Controls</strong
      >.
    </li><li>
      Export cameras and Custom Properties, then test interruption and repeated
      activation.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/blender/scene-events-panel.png"
    alt="FPE Scene Events panel used to activate GardenRevealCamera"
    caption="The SHOW_GARDEN Scene Event activates the named camera and can disable player controls for the duration of the shot."
  />
  <h2>Understand the Godot camera state</h2>
  <h2>Prepare the cameras</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Keep the player camera beneath the player rig so it follows the intended
      motion.
    </li><li>
      Place fixed or cutscene <code>Camera3D</code> nodes at stable scene locations
      and name them by purpose.
    </li><li>Choose one camera as current when the level starts.</li><li>
      Have a coordinator accept camera IDs or references and call <code
        >make_current()</code
      >.
    </li><li>
      Expose a separate <code>return_to_player()</code> operation instead of relying
      on whichever camera happened to be active previously.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <h2>Coordinate control and UI</h2>
  <p>
    Decide whether a camera cut is observational or interactive. For a cutscene,
    disable player input before switching and restore it only after the final
    shot. Keep screen-space UI under a <code>CanvasLayer</code> so it is not accidentally
    tied to a 3D camera. If a transition can be interrupted, make cleanup idempotent:
    returning twice should still leave one current camera and enabled controls.
  </p>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/blender/door-action-timeline.png"
    alt="Blender Action Editor with a short keyed action and a clearly positioned playhead"
    caption="For an animated move, key the shot in Blender and review the timing in the Action Editor before connecting gameplay events."
  />
  <h2>Cut, blend, or animate?</h2>
  <p>
    Use an immediate switch when the edit itself communicates meaning. For a
    smooth move, animate a dedicated transition camera or use a controlled rig;
    do not reparent the player camera mid-shot. Keep gameplay collision and
    player orientation separate from a cinematic camera's transform.
  </p>
  <h2>Common failure checks</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      No view: confirm at least one enabled camera is current and inside the
      active viewport.
    </li><li>
      Player cannot move afterward: check the code path that restores input, not
      only the camera.
    </li><li>
      Camera offset after import: apply or intentionally preserve Blender
      transforms and verify scale.
    </li><li>UI disappears: move screen UI out of the 3D camera hierarchy.</li>
  </ul>
  <Fragment slot="fpe"
    ><p>
      Configure the player camera style in Blender, then add clearly named
      cameras for fixed views and cutscenes. FPE Scene Event commands can
      activate a camera, deactivate player controls for the shot, and explicitly
      reactivate the player camera and controls afterward. Frame events are
      useful when the return must happen at an exact animation beat.
    </p><p>
      <a href="/docs/fpe-cameras.html">Read the FPE camera reference</a>.
    </p></Fragment
  >
</LearningGuideLayout>
